Z||000004||b lo 0||08-25-2001||12:35 AM||||The only way to fix it right is to replace the honeycomb support structure underneath.  Start scouring the junkyards, becase if you can find em, new ones will cost ya!  Lemme guess-there's a bit of a gap between the hood and fascia and it curves up a bit before it gets back to the hood, right?  Pretty common...<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1">'85 GT (in progress)<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">       <BR>White Lightnin!  (NOT moonshine, for all you hillbillies!)</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.vstoreauto.com.com" TARGET=_blank>Audio Imagery</A>, budget-priced car audio supplies from people like Monster Cable, Sony & more!||||reg||1
Z||000005||MinnGreenGT||08-25-2001||12:56 AM||||Just a thought on the sagging front fascia.  Newer cars utilize an expanding foam that can be shaped.  It is cheaper to produce than the honeycomb stuff and probably easier to replace when dented- just spray more in.  I've never tried this, but may before my next repaint (post Stage-2 Kit).  Just get some expanding foam, spray in/around the honeycomb- it WILL grow past the original shape.  Allow it to dry, then cut it with a knife/hacksaw blade/whatever works!  Just a thought.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||08-25-2001||02:03 PM||||If the pastic crush pack is damaged then it needs replacing. The noses will sag even with good crush packs.<P>I mentioned in another thread,,,, you could also try styrofoam. You can get structral insulating foam up to several inches thick. Styrofoam is dimensionally stable to over 200F. You can shape it with almost any kind of cutting tools and coarse abrasives.<P>||||reg||1
